User: in unity, I want to use 2 different assembly definition referencing each other. But I get the error cyclic reference detected. How can I reference them to each other?
<response>In Unity, you can't have two assemblies directly referencing each other due to the cyclic reference error you're encountering. To resolve this issue, you can use interfaces to decouple the assemblies and enable them to communicate without direct references. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do that:

1. Define an interface in Assembly A (let's call it IMyInterface). This interface should contain the methods or properties you want to use in Assembly B.
```csharp
// In Assembly A
public interface IMyInterface
{
    void MyMethod();
}
```
1. In Assembly A, modify the classes that use references from Assembly B to implement the interface you've defined.
```csharp
// In Assembly A
public class MyClassA : IMyInterface
{
    public void MyMethod()
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```
1. In Assembly B, create a class or an adapter that implements the interface defined in Assembly A (IMyInterface). This class will be responsible for handling interactions between the two assemblies.
```csharp
// In Assembly B
public class MyAdapter : IMyInterface
{
    public void MyMethod()
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```
1. Update the code in Assembly A to use the interface-based adapter instead of referencing classes directly from Assembly B.
```csharp
// In Assembly A
public class MyClassA : IMyInterface
{
    private IMyInterface _adapter;

    public MyClassA(IMyInterface adapter)
    {
        _adapter = adapter;
    }

    public void MyMethod()
    {
        _adapter.MyMethod();
    }
}
```
1. In Assembly B, you can now reference Assembly A, but use the adapter to interact with classes in Assembly A.
```csharp
// In Assembly B
public class MyClassB
{
    public void UseAdapter()
    {
        IMyInterface myAdapterInstance = new MyAdapter();
        myAdapterInstance.MyMethod();
    }
}
```
By using interfaces as a bridge for communication between assemblies, you avoid having direct dependencies and can still use the necessary functionality from Assembly B in Assembly A.</response>

